these days just about every person  in your business ask questions about  your data  and the faster they can get answers to  those questions the more efficiently  they can do their jobs  not only can optimizing sql queries keep  all your data consumers happy  but it can also save you money in this  video  we're going to talk through some  strategies for optimizing your bigquery  queries  in our last episode we covered query  execution  including several tools and concepts  that provide the foundation  for many of the optimization techniques  we're highlighting today  if you haven't already i highly  recommend taking a pause to watch it  before diving into this one  alright on to the strategies  one of the most straightforward ways to  optimize a query is to make sure that  you limit the number of bytes it needs  to scan  the first thing you want to do is ensure  you are only selecting the columns that  you really need  that means no select star since bigquery  uses columnar storage  the data scan is proportional to the  number of columns used in the query  if you want to take a peek at the first  few rows of the data to get a feel for  what you're working with  use the preview table feature instead  the next thing you can do is leverage  partitioning and clustering to  automatically prune out data you don't  need in your query  like we talked about in our video on  bigquery storage  this includes leveraging filters or  where clauses  on columns used to partition or cluster  the table itself  all right so we went over some  strategies for minimizing the data  scanned in the query  the next thing we can look closer at is  aggregations  to understand how to optimize  aggregation queries  let's look at how bigquery actually  executes a query that uses  a group by clause first the data is read  from our storage system and group buys  are done  on individual slots then the results  from each slot are bucketed using a hash  function  such that data from the same key ends up  on the same slot  we often refer to this as shuffling  so in this query all partial  aggregations for the same wiki title  end up in the same slot and we can see  the shuffle step happening  in the second stage of the execution  details  further aggregation occurs and the final  result is then passed along to the last  slot  for which limits the results since this  requires a few different aggregation  steps  group by queries can be costly for this  reason you want to ensure that you  aggregate as  late and as seldom as possible this can  come into play for queries that have sub  selects  you'll want to move your aggregation  functions up to the top to avoid  multiple group by executions but there  is an exception  if a table can be reduced drastically by  aggregating  in preparation for being joined then  aggregate it early  another thing you can do to avoid using  group buys is to nest repeated data  for example you might have a  transactions table that has  information about the order including  the items that were purchased  if you use flat denormalization you  would have one row for each ordered item  instead you can create a repeated column  that contains a list of all the items  now if you want to calculate the number  of items in each order  you can use array length and avoid doing  any group buys  great so now you have some tactics for  aggregations  but what about joins let's begin by  looking at how bigquery  executes different types of joins if you  join  two large tables together bigquery  favors what's called a hash join  or a shuffle join similar to the  aggregation we discussed before  bigquery uses a hash function to shuffle  both the left  and the right tables so that the  matching keys end up on the same worker  and then the data can be joined locally  on the other hand if one of your tables  is small enough to fit in memory  it's not necessary to shuffle large  amounts of data  in a small join or a broadcast join  bigquery simply sends the small table to  every single node that's processing the  larger table  now that you have a feel for how  bigquery executes joins  let's consider how you can make queries  that join multiple tables  as efficient as possible first off  you want to make sure you place the  largest table first in the join query  followed by the smallest and then by  decreasing size  next where clauses should be executed as  soon as possible  so that the slots for forming the join  are working with the least amount of  data  you also want to make sure we're using  filters on both  tables like in this example here  this way you're ensuring data you don't  need is being eliminated from both sides  of the join  finally you can cluster or partition  tables based on common join keys  because less data is scanned and the  query will execute faster  and actually clustering improves the  efficiency even more than partitioning  so keep this in mind when deciding on  how to structure your tables  one last way to optimize queries is by  tweaking the way you do filtering and  ordering  something you may not realize is that  when you are using a where clause  bigquery assumes that you have chosen  the best order for your filters  meaning the first part of your where  clause should always contain the filter  that will eliminate the most data  next if you're using an order by  statement you may run into a resources  exceeded error  this is because the final sorting for  your query must be done on a single slot  and if you're attempting to order a very  large result set  the final sorting can overwhelm the slot  that's processing the data  one way you can solve this is by making  sure you use limit  so that the result set is easier to  manage  phew okay we were able to cover a lot of  great techniques  to make your queries more performant  more detail on what we discussed here  plus even more optimization levers are  covered in the links below  so be sure to check them out thanks for  joining us today  and watch out for our next episode of  bigquery spotlight  oh and remember stay curious  [Music]  you
 

